+## Nine mathematical ideas, used over and over
+
+Everything in this track comes back to a short list of topics. Here's the map before the territory — then a closer look at each one, with a worked example.
 
 <div class="topic-grid">
 
 <TopicCard num="01" title="Arithmetic & Algebra">
 
-The foundation everything else sits on: solving for an unknown, rearranging an equation. Every cryptographic protocol is, underneath, a set of algebraic equations that have to hold — ZK proof systems exist to check that they do without revealing the variables that satisfy them.
+The foundation everything else sits on: solving for an unknown, rearranging an equation. Every cryptographic protocol is, underneath, a set of algebraic equations that have to hold.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="02" title="Number Theory">
 
-The study of integers and the relationships between them — divisibility, greatest common divisors, primes. Number theory supplies building blocks like primality and coprimality that almost every other topic on this list depends on.
+The study of integers and the relationships between them — divisibility, greatest common divisors, primes. Supplies building blocks almost every other topic here depends on.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="03" title="Modular Arithmetic">
 
-Arithmetic that wraps around instead of growing forever: $17 \bmod 5 = 2$, the remainder after division. It's often called the language of modern cryptography for a reason — nearly every operation inside a ZK circuit happens modulo some fixed number. Covered in full in the next lesson.
+Arithmetic that wraps around instead of growing forever. Often called the language of modern cryptography — covered in full in the next lesson.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="04" title="Finite Fields">
 
-A number system with a fixed, finite number of elements — for example, the integers $0$ through $6$ under arithmetic modulo $7$. Every ZK proof system runs its arithmetic inside a finite field, because it needs a closed, well-behaved number system rather than the open-ended integers.
+A number system with a fixed, finite number of elements. Every ZK proof system runs its arithmetic inside one, instead of the open-ended integers.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="05" title="Polynomial Mathematics">
 
-Polynomials — expressions like $f(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1$ — are the core building block of modern proof systems. zk-SNARKs, zk-STARKs, and polynomial commitment schemes all ultimately reduce a computation to statements about polynomials.
+The core building block of modern proof systems. zk-SNARKs, zk-STARKs, and polynomial commitment schemes all reduce a computation to statements about polynomials.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="06" title="Linear Algebra">
 
-Vectors and matrices, and the operations that combine them. A circuit's constraints are naturally expressed as matrix equations — exactly how Rank-1 Constraint Systems (R1CS) represent a program before it's turned into a proof.
+Vectors and matrices. A circuit's constraints are naturally expressed as matrix equations — exactly how Rank-1 Constraint Systems (R1CS) represent a program.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="07" title="Probability">
 
-The tool for reasoning about security guarantees in concrete numbers. When a protocol says a cheating prover can only succeed with probability one in a million, that number comes directly from probability theory applied to the protocol's structure.
+The tool for reasoning about security guarantees in concrete numbers, like "a cheating prover succeeds with probability one in a million."
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="08" title="Group Theory">
 
-The study of mathematical structures that follow a specific set of rules — closure, an identity element, inverses. Groups show up everywhere in cryptography, and they're the structure elliptic curve cryptography is built on top of.
+Mathematical structures that follow a specific set of rules — closure, an identity element, inverses. The structure elliptic curve cryptography is built on top of.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 <TopicCard num="09" title="Elliptic Curves">
 
-Curves of the form $y^2 = x^3 + ax + b$, chosen for arithmetic that's easy to compute but hard to reverse. They're the basis of the cryptography behind Bitcoin and Ethereum, and they reappear throughout ZK in pairing-based proof systems.
+Curves chosen for arithmetic that's easy to compute but hard to reverse. The basis of the cryptography behind Bitcoin, Ethereum, and pairing-based proof systems.
 
 </TopicCard>
 
 </div>
 
+### Arithmetic & algebra, worked
+
+Nothing exotic yet — just solving for an unknown:
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$x + 5 = 12 \implies x = 7$$
+
+Every cryptographic protocol is, underneath, a chain of steps like this one — the complexity comes from how many of them get chained together, not from any single step being hard.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Number theory, worked
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$\gcd(12, 18) = 6$$
+
+The greatest common divisor — the largest number that divides both with no remainder. Number theory results like this one decide which numbers can serve as a modulus, and which pairs of numbers have a modular inverse at all (more on that in a couple of lessons).
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Modular arithmetic, a teaser
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$17 \bmod 5 = 2$$
+
+The remainder when $17$ is divided by $5$. This single idea — arithmetic that wraps around instead of growing forever — is the subject of the entire next lesson, because almost everything downstream of it depends on getting comfortable here first.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Finite fields, worked
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$5 + 4 \equiv 2 \pmod{7}$$
+
+Inside the field "modulo $7$," addition wraps back around as soon as it would leave the set $\{0, 1, \dots, 6\}$. Every ZK proof system picks one large prime field like this and does *all* of its arithmetic inside it.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Polynomial mathematics, worked
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$f(x) = x^2 + 2x + 1$$
+
+A polynomial encodes a whole computation as a single algebraic object — evaluate it, add it to another polynomial, or commit to it without revealing it. zk-SNARKs and zk-STARKs are, at their core, elaborate machinery for making statements like these checkable.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Linear algebra, worked
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$\begin{bmatrix} 1 & 2 \end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix} 3 \\ 4 \end{bmatrix} = 1 \cdot 3 + 2 \cdot 4 = 11$$
+
+A single dot product — and, scaled up to thousands of rows, exactly the operation a circuit's constraint system runs to check that a computation was performed correctly.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Probability, worked
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$P(\text{heads}) = \tfrac{1}{2}$$
+
+The simplest possible probability statement — but the same math that lets a protocol say "a cheating prover gets away with it with probability at most $1$ in a million," which is how security guarantees get turned into concrete numbers instead of vague confidence.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Group theory, worked
+
+<Figure caption="Fig. 2 — the integers 0 through 4 under addition, wrapping back to 0 after 4: a cyclic group of order 5.">
+  <CyclicGroupFigure n={5} />
+</Figure>
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$\mathbb{Z}/5\mathbb{Z} = \{0, 1, 2, 3, 4\} \text{ under } +$$
+
+A *group* is just a set with an operation that follows a few simple rules — closure, an identity element, every element has an inverse. The integers mod $5$ under addition are one of the simplest examples. Elliptic curve cryptography is, underneath, a much larger and more useful group built the same way.
+
+</MathBox>
+
+### Elliptic curves, worked
+
+<Figure caption="Fig. 3 — secp256k1: the elliptic curve behind Bitcoin and Ethereum keys.">
+  <EllipticCurveFigure />
+</Figure>
+
+<MathBox label="Worked example">
+
+$$y^2 = x^3 + 7$$
+
+This is secp256k1 — the exact curve used to generate Bitcoin and Ethereum key pairs. Pick a point on the curve, "add" it to itself a secret number of times, and you get a new point that's easy to compute forward but computationally infeasible to reverse. That one-way street is what elliptic curve cryptography — and a large share of pairing-based ZK proof systems — is built on.
+
+</MathBox>
+
 None of this needs to click in a single sitting — nobody picks up finite fields or polynomial commitments on the first pass. This track builds the nine ideas above one lesson at a time, starting with modular arithmetic next, and ties each one back to the cryptography and zero-knowledge systems it actually supports. Once the math stops being abstract and starts being something you can point to in an actual protocol, the rest of ZK stops looking like magic.
